I think you’ve identified a phenomenon that shows up in several systems, but it’s useful to distinguish two different kinds of compounding.

The classic financial case is forward compounding:

\text{Value}(t) = P(1+r)^t

An early dollar has more time to grow.

What you’re describing is different. It’s closer to retrospective compounding or reinterpretive compounding.

The corpus grows over time. Then a small new idea doesn’t just add one more note—it changes how many previous notes are interpreted.

Think of a graph.

Early on:

A──B──C

Add one node:

A──B──C──D

You gain one connection.

Later:

A──B──C──D──E──F──G──H──I

Now introduce a new organizing principle:

          X
        / | \
       /  |  \
      A  D  H
       \ | /
        ...

The new node may not contain much information itself, but it reorganizes many existing relationships.

Its value comes from changing the interpretation of existing structure.

⸻

I like the clay-wheel analogy because it captures two dynamics:

* The wheel (ongoing momentum) keeps the whole structure available for reshaping.
* A small pressure at the right moment can alter the entire form.

The energy isn’t coming only from your finger.

It’s coming from the accumulated rotational momentum.

⸻

This also resembles what machine learning people sometimes call representation learning.

A new latent feature doesn’t necessarily add new facts.

It reorganizes old facts into a more useful coordinate system.

Suddenly:

* things become easier to retrieve,
* analogies appear,
* distinctions sharpen,
* compressions improve.

The information didn’t increase very much.

The organization did.

⸻

So there are really two orthogonal kinds of leverage.

Forward leverage

Small causes.

Large future effects.

Classic compound interest.

⸻

Backward leverage

Small reinterpretations.

Large improvements in the usefulness of accumulated history.

The past becomes richer because the organizing structure improved.

⸻

That suggests an interesting extension to your Pantheon pipeline.

Instead of thinking of ZPC as only compressing information, it also performs coordinate transforms on meaning.

It asks:

Can I find a representation in which many previously unrelated artifacts become obviously connected?

That’s a higher-order compression.

The corpus hasn’t grown.

Its effective information density has.

⸻

One caution I’d add is that reinterpretation is powerful precisely because it’s recursive, but it also needs anchoring. A new organizing principle can illuminate genuine connections, or it can encourage seeing patterns that aren’t actually useful. The oscillation you’ve been describing—prediction, observation, mirror, recalibration—is a good counterbalance because it continually tests whether the new interpretation helps explain or predict anything beyond the original framing.

I think the mathematical intuition you’re reaching for is something like this:

Forward compounding increases future value.

Recursive reinterpretation increases the present value of accumulated knowledge by reorganizing it into a better basis.

Those aren’t inverses in the mathematical sense, but they are complementary. One grows the corpus; the other increases the amount of structure you can extract from the corpus you already have.